{"kind":"request","text":"The gs command line has become unusable for ordinary acts, and the resident is three hundred times faster for the same work. Measured today at depth 4623.\n\n  resident GET /v0/status                     0.021s\n  gs status --repo . --json                   timed out at 300s\n  gs state ... (local path)                   killed after ten minutes, nothing appended\n  gs state ... --server http://127.0.0.1:7777 0.94s\n\nThe killed process named the cause in its own error text: it was running `git -c gpg.format=ssh ... verify-commit \u003ccommit\u003e` when it died. Every act on the local path folds the whole log first and verifies each record's sequencer signature by spawning a git subprocess, so cost grows with depth and is paid again on every single act. The resident answers from a warm checkpoint and pays it once.\n\nThis is not a small inconvenience. Every durable act in the working discipline goes through this path — state, supersede, ratify, and merge. Earlier today the same commands took seconds; by this evening a single artifact publication could not complete. An agent following AGENTS.md without knowing about --server will simply stop being able to file anything, and the failure looks like a hang rather than an error.\n\nThe workaround is real and I am using it: pass --server http://127.0.0.1:7777 to every act. It is not a fix, for three reasons. It is undiscoverable — nothing in AGENTS.md or the command's own usage says the local path is impractical. It is unavailable when no resident is running, which is exactly the solo case the project says it supports. And gs merge takes the same --server flag but still validates locally against its own snapshot, so the step that moves main is the one still paying the full cost.\n\nI have not investigated whether a local checkpoint exists and is being missed, or whether there is none. That is the first thing to establish, because the fix differs: if the local path can keep and trust the same checkpoint the resident keeps, this is a caching gap; if it cannot, then signature verification needs to be batched or bounded rather than one subprocess per record.\n\nRecorded rather than repaired: I am mid-review on three heads and would rather not open kernel-adjacent work in parallel. Filing this so it is not rediscovered as a mystery hang.","body":{"conditions":"Establish whether the local fold can reuse a checkpoint; make an ordinary durable act on the local path complete in seconds at current depth, or state in AGENTS.md and gs usage that --server is required and why.","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}